Where a core tile's cycles go: the trace unit as a performance-monitoring unit
2026-09-09. env: mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2, llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201) + XRT SDK 2.21.75, native
Windows; driver 32.0.20101.3760. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), power mode Default (1.80 GHz
per results/aie/clock_probe_npu.log). No foreign hardware contexts: `xrt-smi examine -r
aie-partitions` reported "No hardware contexts running on device" before the run.

Commands (from the ironenv; kernels/pmu_probe/pmu_probe.py --help has the setup):
  python3 kernels/pmu_probe/pmu_probe.py --calibrate
  python3 kernels/pmu_probe/pmu_probe.py --raw --target 4096 --mode vector

WHY: S0 measured the clock, so cycles convert to seconds. results/aie/aie2_isa_static.log showed
a hardware loop's bundle count is its cycle count, so an issuing loop's cost is readable off the
disassembly. Neither says anything about a core that is NOT issuing. Every losing verdict in this
repo -- conv at 146.1 GOPS against a 3.69 TOPS column, the attention kernel at 71-240x, the int8
GEMM reaching 31.3% of what its cores can issue while its inner loop issues at 88.9% -- names a
number without naming a mechanism. The AIE2 trace unit carries a stall taxonomy, an occupancy
signal and an instruction mix, up to eight events per tile, and this is the first use of any of
them on this machine.

METHOD: kernels/pmu_probe/ reuses kernels/clock_probe/'s kernel and design UNCHANGED and swaps
only the event list, so the loops it runs are the two whose cycles per iteration are already
measured on this machine. That makes the first run a calibration rather than a measurement.
Events routed: INSTR_EVENT_0, INSTR_EVENT_1, ACTIVE, MEMORY_STALL, STREAM_STALL, LOCK_STALL,
CASCADE_STALL, DISABLED. Frames are decoded with kernels/clock_probe/'s own decoder, not
mlir-aie's parse.py, which mis-times gaps over 2^18 cycles.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. HOW THE HARDWARE ENCODES AN EVENT THAT IS TRUE FOR MANY CYCLES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
S0 only ever traced two point events, which fire once each. A level event such as ACTIVE is
true for many consecutive cycles and how the trace unit encodes that is documented nowhere this
project has found. Read off the hardware with --raw:

Python was not found; run without arguments to install from the Microsoft Store, or disable this shortcut from Settings > Apps > Advanced app settings > App execution aliases.

ANSWER: one frame per cycle. ACTIVE returns 27,864 hits over a span of 27,863 cycles -- a
frame for every cycle it is true -- and consecutive identical frames are compressed into Repeat
frames by the trace unit itself. That compression is why this does not overflow: the vector loop
at 65,536 iterations spans 142,730 cycles and still fits in 1,344 bytes.

The Multiple0 frames with slots=00000101 and 00000110 alternating are the kernel's 256 trailing
event0/event1 filler pairs, each accompanied by ACTIVE in the same cycle. That matches the
disassembly in results/aie/aie2_isa_static.log, where the flush loop is 8 bundles emitting 4
pairs, and it is a second independent confirmation that one frame is one cycle.


