--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. WHAT THE NUMBERS SAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Cycles per iteration converge on the measured values from above as the loop grows -- 2.0051 ->
2.0013 -> 2.0003 and 9.0020 -> 9.0005 -> 9.0001 -- because the fixed loop-entry cost amortises.
The instrument reproduces both of S0's loops.

The accounting is the stronger result. Subtracting the traced stall cycles and the loop's own
cycles from the cycles the core was alive leaves EXACTLY 190 cycles on every vector run and
EXACTLY 198 on every scalar run, across a 16x range of work. A residual that is constant rather
than proportional is the kernel's prologue and epilogue, and it is what says the decomposition

    cycles the core is alive = issuing + memory + stream + lock + cascade stalls

closes on this hardware. ACTIVE is inclusive of stall cycles, not exclusive of them: a core
waiting on a lock is still enabled and not halted, so issuing cycles are what remains after the
stalls are subtracted rather than something the hardware reports directly.

LOCK_STALL is the only non-zero stall term in any of these runs, and it is large. The core waits
roughly 8,500 to 12,700 cycles per dispatch on the input ObjectFifo's lock -- about 5 to 7
microseconds at 1.80 GHz -- and that figure barely moves as the loop grows from 4,096 to 65,536
iterations, so it is a fixed cost of getting data to the core and not a function of the work.
On the shortest run it is 18,926 cycles against 8,915 cycles of issuing: the core spends 68% of
its life waiting and 32% computing, on a kernel whose inner loop the disassembly rates as
perfectly scheduled.

That is the mechanism this repo has been inferring from throughput fits and can now measure. It
also sharpens results/aie/dispatch_floor_npu.log's 169.8 microsecond hardware floor: some of
that floor is visible from inside the core as lock wait, and the rest is outside the core
entirely, because 10,000 cycles is only about 3% of 169.8 microseconds.

MEMORY_STALL, STREAM_STALL and CASCADE_STALL are zero throughout, which is correct for this
design -- it has one core, no cascade, no streams beyond the two ObjectFifos, and a loop whose
operands are in registers. They are not shown to work by this run. A kernel that uses them is
needed before any of those three can be trusted, and that is the next step.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Only LOCK_STALL has been seen non-zero. Three of the four stall categories are unexercised.
- One core tile, logical Tile(0,2), physical (2,1). One power mode.
- The traced window starts when the trace unit is enabled, not when the dispatch begins, so the
  cycles the core was alive are not the whole submit-to-wait bracket and should not be compared
  to it directly.
- ACTIVE being inclusive of stalls is inferred from the accounting closing, not from a document.
- The 8-event budget per tile is a hardware limit; a wider taxonomy needs more than one run.
